# Chapter 10 · Women's Political Status and Climate Vulnerability

> Political empowerment is one of the four sub-indices of the Global Gender Gap (2026 Q4), and climate impacts appeared in 2026 Q5 (answered in Topic 4). This chapter gives the essentials for status questions; Topic 6 covers women and governance in full.

## 10.1  Women's political status

::: data Women in politics in Pakistan | latest figures
- The National Assembly has ==336== seats: 266 general, ==60== reserved for women and 10 for non-Muslims.
- In the ==2024== general election, **306** women contested general seats and only ==11== won.
- In ==2025== the share of women among federal ministers fell to **zero** (Global Gender Gap Report 2025).
- **Maryam Nawaz** became the first woman **chief minister** (Punjab) in February ==2024==.
- Pakistan ranked ==107th== on **political empowerment** in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026.
:::

Key facts:

1. **Reserved seats** — 60 in the National Assembly and 17 in the Senate since ==2002==; filled by party lists in proportion to general seats won.
2. **Firsts** — **Benazir Bhutto**, first woman prime minister of a Muslim-majority country (==1988==); **Fahmida Mirza**, first woman Speaker (==2008==).
3. **The Elections Act 2017** requires parties to give at least **5%** of general-seat tickets to women, and allows the Election Commission to declare void a constituency result where women's turnout is below **10%**.
4. **Women voters** — millions fewer women than men are registered to vote, largely because of missing CNICs; registration drives have narrowed the gap.
5. **Barriers** — family and tribal opposition, the cost of campaigns, party patronage, harassment, and agreements in some areas to stop women voting.

## 10.1a  The women-voter gap and local government

Millions fewer women than men are on the electoral rolls, mainly because many women lack **CNICs**; the Election Commission, NADRA and civil-society groups have run registration drives to close this gap. In some areas, local agreements among elders have tried to stop women voting — which is why the **Elections Act 2017** lets the Election Commission void results where women's turnout is under **10%**. At the local level, the **devolution plan** of ==2000–01== reserved **33%** of local-council seats for women, bringing tens of thousands of women into elected office for the first time; later provincial local-government laws reduced this share in several provinces.

## 10.1b  Women in the bureaucracy and judiciary

Women's status also depends on their presence in the **state machinery**. The **10% quota** in federal services, together with rising female education, has increased the number of women officers in the civil service, including in the police and foreign service. In the judiciary, women have been appointed as judges of the high courts and, since ==2022==, of the **Supreme Court**. Yet women remain a small minority in senior posts. More women in decision-making positions in the bureaucracy, police and courts improves the handling of cases involving women — for example, complaints of violence and harassment.

## 10.2  Why political status matters for other dimensions

Women legislators, especially through the cross-party **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==), drove many of the laws of 2010–2016 — on harassment, acid attacks, anti-women practices and honour crimes. Without women in power, health, education and employment policies are less likely to address women's needs.

::: fig cycle | How political voice links to other dimensions of status.
@centre Women's political voice
Laws on violence and property
Budgets for girls' schools and clinics
Workplace protections and childcare
Enforcement and accountability
Role models for girls
:::

## 10.3  Climate change and women's status

Pakistan produces **less than 1%** of global emissions but is among the countries most affected by climate change. Climate shocks worsen every dimension of women's status:

- **Health:** the ==2022== floods left about ==650,000== pregnant women in affected areas (UNFPA); heat waves raise risks in pregnancy.
- **Education:** thousands of schools were damaged; girls are more likely to drop out after disasters.
- **Employment:** women lost livestock, crops and home-based work; they own little land to recover.
- **Law and protection:** relief registered to male heads of household and requiring CNICs left many women out; reports of early marriage after disasters.

Topic 4, Chapter 14 analyses climate impacts through ecofeminism and feminist environmentalism.

## 10.3a  Heat waves and women's status

Recurrent heat waves — with temperatures near or above 50°C in cities such as **Jacobabad** — affect women in specific ways. Women cooking over open fires in poorly ventilated kitchens, and women working in fields during harvests and cotton picking, face serious heat stress. **Pregnant** women face higher risks of complications. As water sources dry up, the time women spend fetching water grows. Power cuts during heat waves make homes hotter, and home-based workers lose income when they cannot work. Heat-action plans rarely consider these gendered risks.

## 10.3b  Barriers to women's political status — and what would change them

Women's political status in Pakistan is limited at every stage (Topic 6 covers this in depth):

1. **As voters:** about **10 million** fewer women than men were registered in ==2024==, mainly because many women lack identity cards; women's turnout was about **43%** against **52%** for men.
2. **As candidates:** parties give women few winnable tickets; only **11** women won general National Assembly seats in ==2024==, despite the **5%** ticket rule of the Elections Act 2017.
3. **As representatives:** most women legislators sit on **reserved seats** filled from party lists, so they depend on party leaders and lack constituencies.
4. **As leaders:** in ==2025== there were **no women federal ministers** — a key reason for Pakistan's low political-empowerment score in the Global Gender Gap Report.

**What would change it:** universal identity cards for women; enforcement of the 10% turnout rule; a stronger candidate quota in winnable seats; direct election of women's seats; and women in cabinet and senior posts. Because the political sub-index is the one that can move fastest — as the ==2026== rise of 11 places showed — political reform is the quickest way to raise women's measured status, though not a substitute for change in health, education and work.

## 10.3c  Climate adaptation with women at the centre

Women are not only victims of climate change but **agents** of adaptation. Rural women manage water, fuel, seeds and livestock and hold knowledge about local environments. Effective adaptation therefore includes women in **disaster committees**, gives them early warnings by mobile phone and radio, registers them directly for **relief and compensation** (rather than through male household heads), builds flood shelters with separate facilities for women, and supports climate-resilient livelihoods such as kitchen gardens and home-based enterprises. Pakistan's **Climate Change Gender Action Plan** (==2022==) commits the state to such measures; the challenge is implementation.

## 10.4  Putting the dimensions together

::: fig mapping | The dimensions of women's status and their key laws or programmes.
@left Dimension
@right Key law or programme
Health => Lady Health Workers (1994); Sehat card; BISP nutrition
Education => Article 25A (2010); girls' stipends
Employment => Harassment Act 2010 (amended 2022); Sindh Home-Based Workers Act 2018
Family law => MFLO 1961; child-marriage laws (Sindh 2013, Islamabad 2025)
Protection => Women's Protection Act 2006; honour law 2016; Anti-Rape Act 2021
Politics => 60 reserved seats (2002); Elections Act 2017
Digital => PECA 2016; digital BISP payments
:::

::: eye | Improving women's status "as a whole" (2023)
The 2023 question asked for measures to improve women's status "**as a whole**". Link the dimensions: **education** raises health and earnings; **health** enables work; **work** gives voice and bargaining power; **law** protects; **political voice** turns needs into policy. The best answers show these links, using this chapter's mapping figure.
:::
